  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "dissect_rtps",
    "file": "wireshark/epan/dissectors/packet-rtps.c",
    "description": "`dissect_rtps` stores a `guid_copy` allocated with `wmem_memdup(wmem_packet_scope(), ...)` in `pinfo->private_table`, which is created with `g_free` destroy callbacks. When another RTPS message is dissected using the same packet-info table, insertion under the same constant `RTPS_TCPMAP_DOMAIN_ID_KEY_STR` replaces the prior entry, and GLib immediately `g_free`s the old packet-scope `guid_copy`. The wmem packet allocator still tracks that allocation and frees it again in `wmem_simple_free_all` when packet scope is left, causing the observed double-free. The allocation/free ownership mismatch at lines 11930-11938 is the root cause, rather than packet-scope cleanup.",
    "conditions": "The input must be decoded as TCP and cause at least two valid, non-ping RTPS/RTPX headers to reach `dissect_rtps` within one packet dissection while sharing the same non-NULL `pinfo->private_table`. Each invocation inserts a participant GUID under the same constant string key. The second insertion replaces and frees the first packet-scope value through the hash table's `g_free` value destructor; leaving packet scope then frees that same value again. The PoV contains repeated RTPS/RTPX-like content in the TCP payload that produces this repeated insertion path."
  },
